= #text("Corn-Pone Opinions / 玉米饼式的意见")

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("Fifty years ago, when I was a boy of fifteen and helping to inhabit a Missourian village on the banks of the Mississippi, I had a friend whose society was very dear to me because I was forbidden by my mother to partake of it. He was a gay and impudent and satirical and delightful young black man--a slave--who daily preached sermons from the top of his master’s woodpile, with me for sole audience. He imitated the pulpit style of the several clergymen of the village, and did it well, and with fine passion and energy. To me he was a wonder. I believed he was the greatest orator in the United States and would some day be heard from. But it did not happen; in the distribution of rewards he was overlooked. It is the way, in this world.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("五十年前，当我还是一个十五岁的男孩，在密西西比河畔的一个密苏里村庄帮忙居住时，我有一个朋友，他的社交对我来说非常珍贵，因为我的母亲禁止我参与其中。他是一个快乐、无礼、讽刺、令人愉快的年轻黑人——一个奴隶——每天在他主人的柴堆顶上布道，而我是唯一的听众。 He imitated the pulpit style of the several clergymen of the village, and did it well, and with fine passion and energy.对我来说他是一个奇迹。 I believed he was the greatest orator in the United States and would some day be heard from.但它并没有发生；在奖励的分配中，他被忽视了。这就是这个世界的方式。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("He interrupted his preaching, now and then, to saw a stick of wood; but the sawing was a pretense--he did it with his mouth; exactly imitating the sound the bucksaw makes in shrieking its way through the wood. But it served its purpose; it kept his master from coming out to see how the work was getting along. I listened to the sermons from the open window of a lumber room at the back of the house. One of his texts was this:")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("他时不时地中断讲道，看到一根木棍。但锯锯是假装的——他是用嘴锯的。完全模仿了钢锯在木材中尖叫时发出的声音。但它达到了目的；这使得他的主人无法出来查看工作进展情况。我在房子后面一间杂物间开着的窗户里听布道。他的一篇文字是这样的：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("“You tell me whar a man gits his corn pone, en I’ll tell you what his ’pinions is.“")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("“你告诉我一个人怎么吃玉米棒，我就告诉你他的‘想法’是什么。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("I can never forget it. It was deeply impressed upon me. By my mother. Not upon my memory, but elsewhere. She had slipped in upon me while I was absorbed and not watching. The black philosopher’s idea was that a man is not independent, and cannot afford views which might interfere with his bread and butter. If he would prosper, he must train with the majority; in matters of large moment, like politics and religion, he must think and feel with the bulk of his neighbors, or suffer damage in his social standing and in his business prosperities. He must restrict himself to corn-pone opinions--at least on the surface. He must get his opinions from other people; he must reason out none for himself; he must have no first-hand views.")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("我永远无法忘记它。这给我留下了深刻的印象。由我母亲。不是在我的记忆中，而是在别处。当我全神贯注、没有注意时，她溜到了我身边。这位黑人哲学家的想法是，一个人不是独立的，不能承受可能干扰他的面包和黄油的观点。如果他想成功，就必须与大多数人一起训练；在政治和宗教等重大问题上，他必须与大多数邻居一起思考和感受，否则他的社会地位和商业繁荣就会受到损害。他必须限制自己的观点——至少在表面上如此。他必须听取别人的意见；他不能为自己推理出任何事情；他一定没有第一手的意见。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("I think Jerry was right, in the main, but I think he did not go far enough.")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("我认为杰里基本上是对的，但我认为他做得还不够。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("1. It was his idea that a man conforms to the majority view of his locality by calculation and intention.")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("1. 他认为一个人通过算计和意图来符合当地大多数人的观点。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("This happens, but I think it is not the rule.")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("这种情况确实发生了，但我认为这不是规则。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("2. It was his idea that there is such a thing as a first-hand opinion; an original opinion; an opinion which is coldly reasoned out in a man’s head, by a searching analysis of the facts involved, with the heart unconsulted, and the jury room closed against outside influences. It may be that such an opinion has been born somewhere, at some time or other, but I suppose it got away before they could catch it and stuff it and put it in the museum.")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("2. 他认为存在第一手意见；原创意见；一种观点是在一个人的头脑中冷酷地推理出来的，通过对所涉及的事实进行深入分析，不征求内心的意见，并且陪审团室对外界影响关闭。也许这样的观点已经在某个地方、某个时间或其他地方诞生了，但我想在他们抓住它、​​把它塞进博物馆之前它就已经消失了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("I am persuaded that a coldly-thought-out and independent verdict upon a fashion in clothes, or manners, or literature, or politics, or religion, or any other matter that is projected into the field of our notice and interest, is a most rare thing--if it has indeed ever existed.")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("我相信，对服装、礼仪、文学、政治、宗教或任何其他引起我们注意和兴趣的事物的时尚进行冷静思考和独立的判断是一件极其罕见的事情——如果它确实存在过的话。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S010")[#text("A new thing in costume appears--the flaring hoopskirt, for example--and the passers-by are shocked, and the irreverent laugh. Six months later everybody is reconciled; the fashion has established itself; itit is admired, now, and no one laughs. Public opinion resented it before, public opinion accepts it now, and is happy in it. Why? Was the resentment reasoned out? Was the acceptance reasoned out? No. The instinct that moves to conformity did the work. It is our nature to conform; it is a force which not many can successfully resist. What is its seat? The inborn requirement of self-approval. We all have to bow to that; there are no exceptions. Even the woman who refuses from first to last to wear the hoopskirt comes under that law and is its slave; she could not wear the skirt and have her own approval; and that she must have, she cannot help herself. But as a rule our self-approval has its source in but one place and not elsewhere--the approval of other people. A person of vast consequences can introduce any kind of novelty in dress and the general world will presently adopt it--moved to do it, in the first place, by the natural instinct to passively yield to that vague something recognized as authority, and in the second place by the human instinct to train with the multitude and have its approval. An empress introduced the hoopskirt, and we know the result. A nobody introduced the bloomer, and we know the result. If Eve should come again, in her ripe renown, and reintroduce her quaint styles--well, we know what would happen. And we should be cruelly embarrassed, along at first.")]

#zh(id: "S010")[#text("一种新的服装出现了——例如喇叭裙——路人感到震惊，并发出不敬的笑声。六个月后，大家都和解了；时尚已经确立了自己的地位；现在它受到赞赏，但没有人嘲笑它。以前舆论反感，现在舆论接受，乐在其中。为什么？怨恨是否得到合理解释？接受的理由是否合理？不。趋于一致的本能起了作用。顺从是我们的本性；这是一种没有多少人能够成功抵抗的力量。它的座位是什么？自我认可的天生要求。我们都必须屈服于这一点；没有例外。即使是自始至终拒绝穿箍裙的女人也受该法律管辖，成为其奴隶。她不能穿这条裙子并得到她自己的认可；她必须这样做，但她无法控制自己。但一般来说，我们的自我认可只有一个地方而不是其他地方——其他人的认可。一个具有巨大影响力的人可以在着装上引入任何一种新奇的东西，而整个世界很快就会采用它——这样做，首先是出于自然本能，被动地屈服于那种被认为是权威的模糊事物，其次是出于人类本能，即与大众一起训练并获得大众的认可。一位皇后引入了箍裙，我们知道结果。没有人介绍灯笼花，我们知道结果。如果夏娃以她成熟的名气再次出现，并重新引入她古朴的风格——好吧，我们知道会发生什么。我们应该感到残酷的尴尬，起初。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("The hoopskirt runs its course and disappears. Nobody reasons about it. One woman abandons the fashion; her neighbor notices this and follows her lead; this influences the next woman; and so on and so on, and presently the skirt has vanished out of the world, no one knows how nor why; nor cares, for that matter. It will come again, by and by; and in due course will go again.")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("箍裙自然而然地消失了。没有人对此有任何理由。一个女人放弃了时尚；她的邻居注意到了这一点并跟随了她的领导。这会影响下一个女人；等等等等，不久这条裙子就从世界上消失了，没有人知道如何或为什么；就此而言，也不关心。渐渐地，它还会再来；并在适当的时候会再次去。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("Twenty-five years ago, in England, six or eight wine glasses stood grouped by each person’s plate at a dinner party, and they were used, not left idle and empty; to-day there are but three or four in the group, and the average guest sparingly uses about two of them. We have not adopted this new fashion yet, but we shall do it presently. We shall not think it out; we shall merely conform, and let it go at that. We get our notions and habits and opinions from outside influences; we do not have to study them out.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("二十五年前，在英国的一次晚宴上，六到八个酒杯按每个人的盘子排列在一起，并且它们被使用，而不是闲置或空着;如今，团体中只有三四个人，一般客人很少使用其中的两个。我们还没有采用这种新方式，但我们很快就会采用。我们不会想清楚；我们只是顺从，然后就这样吧。我们的观念、习惯和意见都是从外界影响中获得的；我们不必研究它们。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S013")[#text("Our table manners, and company manners, and street manners change from time to time, but the changes are not reasoned out; we merely notice and conform. We are creatures of outside influences; as a rule we do not think, we only imitate. We cannot invent standards that will stick; what we mistake for standards are only fashions, and perishable. We may continue to admire them, but we drop the use of them. We notice this in literature. Shakespeare is a standard, and fifty years ago we used to write tragedies which we couldn’t tell from--from somebody else’s; but we don’t do it any more, now. Our prose standard, three quarters of a century ago, was ornate and diffuse; some authority or other changed it in the direction of compactness and simplicity, and conformity followed, without argument. The historical novel starts up suddenly, and sweeps the land. Everybody writes one, and the nation is glad. We had historical novels before; but nobody read them, and the rest of us conformed--without reasoning it out. We are conforming in the other way, now, because it is another case of everybody.")]

#zh(id: "S013")[#text("我们的餐桌礼仪、公司礼仪、街头礼仪时常发生变化，但这些变化并不是理性的；我们只是注意到并遵守。我们是受外界影响的生物；通常我们不会思考，我们只会模仿。我们无法发明长期有效的标准；我们误以为的标准只是时尚，而且容易消逝。我们可能会继续钦佩它们，但我们会放弃使用它们。我们在文学中注意到这一点。莎士比亚是一个标准，五十年前，我们常常写出我们无法辨别的悲剧——与别人的悲剧；但现在我们不再这样做了。四分之三个世纪前，我们的散文标准是华丽而散漫的。某些权威人士或其他人将其朝着紧凑和简单的方向进行了改变，并且毫无争议地遵循了一致性。历史小说突然兴起，席卷大地。每个人都写一篇，全国都很高兴。我们以前有历史小说；但没有人读过它们，我们其他人也纷纷效仿——没有推理出来。现在，我们以另一种方式顺从，因为这是每个人的另一个情况。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S014")[#text("The outside influences are always pouring in upon us, and we are always obeying their orders and accepting their verdicts. The Smiths like the new play; the Joneses go to see it, and they copy the Smith verdict. Morals, religions, politics, get their following from surrounding influences and atmospheres, almost entirely; not from study, not from thinking. A man must and will have his own approval first of all, in each and every moment and circumstance of his life--even if he must repent of a self-approved act the moment after its commission, in order to get his self-approval again: but, speaking in general terms, a man’s self-approval in the large concerns of life has its source in the approval of the peoples about him, and not in a searching personal examination of the matter. Mohammedans are Mohammedans because they are born and reared among that sect, not because they have thought it out and can furnish sound reasons for being Mohammedans; we know why Catholics are Catholics; why Presbyterians are Presbyterians; why Baptists are Baptists; why Mormons are Mormons; why thieves are thieves; why monarchists are monarchists; why Republicans are Republicans and Democrats, Democrats. We know it is a matter of association and sympathy, not reasoning and examination; that hardly a man in the world has an opinion upon morals, politics, or religion which he got otherwise than through his associations and sympathies. Broadly speaking, there are none but corn-pone opinions. And broadly speaking, corn-pone stands for self-approval. Self-approval is acquired mainly from the approval of other people. The result is conformity. Sometimes conformity has a sordid business interest--the bread-and-butter interest--but not in most cases, I think. I think that in the majority of cases it is unconscious and not calculated; that it is born of the human being’s natural yearning to stand well with his fellows and have their inspiring approval and praise--a yearning which is commonly so strong and so insistent that it cannot be effectually resisted, and must have its way.")]

#zh(id: "S014")[#text("外界的影响总是涌向我们，我们总是服从他们的命令，接受他们的裁决。史密斯一家喜欢这个新剧；琼斯一家去看了，他们复制了史密斯的判决。道德、宗教、政治几乎完全是从周围的影响和氛围中获得追随者；不是来自学习，不是来自思考。一个人在生命中的每时每刻都必须并且将会首先得到自己的认可——即使他必须在自我认可的行为实施后立即后悔，以便再次获得自我认可：但是，一般而言，一个人在生活的重大问题上的自我认可源于周围人民的认可，而不是来自对此事的彻底的个人审视。伊斯兰教徒之所以是伊斯兰教徒，是因为他们在该教派中出生和长大，而不是因为他们深思熟虑并能为成为伊斯兰教徒提供充分的理由；相反，他们是穆斯林。我们知道为什么天主教徒是天主教徒；为什么长老会是长老会；为什么浸信会是浸信会；为什么摩门教徒是摩门教徒；为什么小偷是小偷；为什么君主主义者是君主主义者；为什么共和党是共和党，民主党是民主党。我们知道这是一个联想和同情的问题，而不是推理和检验的问题；世界上几乎没有人对道德、政治或宗教的看法是通过交往和同情获得的。从广义上讲，除了统一的意见之外，没有其他的意见。从广义上讲，玉米饼代表自我认可。自我认可主要通过审批获得其他人的。结果是一致。有时，从众会带来肮脏的商业利益——面包和黄油的利益——但我认为在大多数情况下并非如此。我认为，大多数情况下，这是无意识的，不是经过计算的；它源于人类与他人和谐共处并获得他们鼓舞人心的认可和赞扬的自然渴望——这种渴望通常如此强烈和持久，以至于无法有效抵制，并且必须以自己的方式进行。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S015")[#text("A political emergency brings out the corn-pone opinion in fine force in its two chief varieties--the pocketbook variety, which has its origin in self-interest, and the bigger variety, the sentimental variety--the one which can’t bear to be outside the pale; can’t bear to be in disfavor; can’t endure the averted face and the cold shoulder; wants to stand well with his friends, wants to be smiled upon, wants to be welcome, wants to hear the precious words, “He’s on the right track!” Uttered, perhaps by an ass, but still an ass of high degree, an ass whose approval is gold and diamonds to a smaller ass, and confers glory and honor and happiness, and membership in the herd. For these gauds many a man will dump his life-long principles into the street, and his conscience along with them. We have seen it happen. In some millions of instances.")]

#zh(id: "S015")[#text("政治紧急情况会以两种主要形式展现出民众的意见：一种是出于自身利益的钱包型意见，另一种是更大的意见，即感伤性的意见，即无法忍受被抛在外的意见；一种是情绪化的意见。不忍失宠；受不了侧脸和冷肩；想要和朋友们相处融洽，想要被微笑，想要受到欢迎，想要听到珍贵的话语：“他走在正确的道路上！”也许是一头驴子说出来的，但仍然是一头高级别的驴子，一头驴子的认可是对较小的驴子的黄金和钻石，并赋予荣耀、荣誉和幸福，以及牛群中的成员资格。为了这些花哨的东西，许多人会将他一生的原则抛到街上，连他的良知也随之抛到街上。我们已经看到它发生了。在数百万个实例中。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S016")[#text("Men think they think upon great political questions, and they do; but they think with their party, not independently; they read its literature, but not that of the other side; they arrive at convictions, but they are drawn from a partial view of the matter in hand and are of no particular value. They swarm with their party, they feel with their party, they are happy in their party’s approval; and where the party leads they will follow, whether for right and honor, or through blood and dirt and a mush of mutilated morals.")]

#zh(id: "S016")[#text("人们认为他们思考了重大的政治问题，而且他们也确实这样做了。但他们与党派一起思考，而不是独立思考；他们读其文献，但不读对方的文献；它们得出了信念，但它们是从对当前问题的片面观点出发的，没有特别的价值。他们蜂拥而至，他们与自己的政党同在，他们为自己的政党的认可而感到高兴；党领导到哪里，他们就会追随哪里，无论是为了权利和荣誉，还是通过鲜血、污垢和残破的道德。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S017")[#text("In our late canvass half of the nation passionately believed that in silver lay salvation, the other half as passionately believed that that way lay destruction. Do you believe that a tenth part of the people, on either side, had any rational excuse for having an opinion about the matter at all? I studied that mighty question to the bottom--came out empty. Half of our people passionately believe in high tariff, the other half believe otherwise. Does this mean study and examination, or only feeling? The latter, I think. I have deeply studied that question, too--and didn’t arrive. We all do no end of feeling, and we mistake it for thinking. And out of it we get an aggregation which we consider a boon. Its name is Public Opinion. It is held in reverence. It settles everything. Some think it the Voice of God.")]

#zh(id: "S017")[#text("在我们最近的游说中，一半的人热情地相信白银带来救赎，另一半则热情地相信白银带来毁灭。您是否相信双方都有十分之一的人有任何合理的理由对此事发表意见？我彻底研究了这个重大问题——结果却一无所获。我们一半的人热衷于高关税，另一半则不这么认为。这是学习和考试，还是只是感觉？我认为是后者。我也深入研究过这个问题——但没有得出结论。我们都有无穷无尽的感觉，却误认为它是思考。由此我们得到了一个我们认为是福音的聚合。它的名字是舆论。它受到人们的崇敬。它解决了一切。有些人认为这是上帝的声音。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S018")[#text("Errors deemed most likely to be the printer’s have been corrected, and are noted here. The references are to the page and line in the original.")]

#zh(id: "S018")[#text("被认为最有可能是打印机的错误已得到纠正，并在此处注明。参考文献是原文中的页码和行。")]

